I verified the inter-network path between the “desktop” subnet (172.16.10.0/24) and the “laptop” subnet (172.16.20.0/24) that are joined by the inter-VLAN gateway on router0. From PC4 (172.16.10.4) I can ping Laptop0 (172.16.20.10) successfully (0% loss, ~2.4 ms), and Laptop0 can ping PC4 as well. The TTL values (~63) indicate a single routed hop as expected for inter-VLAN traffic. On router0, FRR shows both networks are directly connected on subinterfaces eth0.10 (172.16.10.0/24) and eth0.20 (172.16.20.0/24), and both hosts have correct defaults (PC4 via 172.16.10.1, Laptop0 via 172.16.20.1). This confirms the “box that joins the two halves” (router0’s inter-VLAN routing) is operating normally and the path is healthy
